<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Neck Pain: Best Points" description="Learn how to use acupressure for neck pain relief. Guide to GB21, GB20, LI4, and other points that release neck tension and restore mobility." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Neck Pain", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-neck-pain/" }]} datePublished="2025-04-15" dateModified="2026-06-01" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Neck Pain: Best Points for Fast Relief</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Many people turn to acupressure for neck pain because several of the key points sit right in the muscles that become tense and painful. The evidence for acupressure on musculoskeletal pain is promising but still limited in quality, and the routines below are a practical, commonly taught way to release neck tension at home.</p><section id="gb21" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gb21-jianjing-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">1. GB21 (Jianjing) - Shoulder Well</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GB21 is the primary point used for neck and shoulder tension in traditional Chinese medicine practice. It sits in the trapezius muscle, which is often a major source of the discomfort.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> At the highest point of the shoulder, midway between the neck and the shoulder joint.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Use your opposite hand to press deeply into the shoulder muscle. Hold for 1-2 minutes while slowly turning your head side to side.</p></section><section id="gb20" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">2. GB20 (Fengchi) - Wind Pool</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GB20 is a standard point for releasing tension at the base of the skull and upper neck, where many people hold stress. <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/">GB20 guide</a>.</p></section><section id="si3" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">3. SI3 (Houxi) - Back Ravine</h2><p class="mb-3">SI3 is a point traditionally used for neck pain and for acute neck stiffness, sometimes called wry neck.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the side of the hand, at the end of the pinky finger crease when the hand is in a loose fist.</p></section><section id="li4" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">4. LI4 (Hegu) - Joining Valley</a></h2><p class="mb-3">LI4 is a distal point traditionally used to treat pain in the head and neck area. It is one of the most commonly tested points in headache and pain research. <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/">LI4 guide</a>.</p></section><section id="routine" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Neck Pain Relief Routine</h2><ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li>Press GB21 on both shoulders for 2 minutes each</li><li>Stimulate GB20 at the skull base for 2 minutes</li><li>Press SI3 on both hands for 1 minute each</li><li>Finish with LI4 on both hands for 1 minute each</li><li>Gently stretch neck by tilting ear toward each shoulder</li></ol></section><section id="evidence" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Scientific Evidence</h2><p class="mb-3">Review evidence for acupressure on musculoskeletal pain, including neck pain, is generally positive but limited in quality. Reviews of acupressure for pain (Chen YW &amp; Wang HH, 2014, PMID 23415783) and for symptom relief more broadly (Lee EJ &amp; Frazier SK, 2011, PMID 21531533) describe the evidence as promising while noting that many studies are small or hard to blind. Work in this area commonly uses points at the base of the skull (GB20), the shoulder (GB21), and the neck and upper back, including points such as <a href="/points/si9-jianzhen-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">SI9 (Jianzhen)</a> and <a href="/points/bl10-tianzhu-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">BL10 (Tianzhu)</a>. No reliable percentage reduction in neck pain is yet established. Treat the routine above as a complement to, not a replacement for, professional neck care.</p></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> Consult a healthcare provider for neck pain following trauma, accompanied by numbness/tingling in arms, or persistent despite self-care.</p></div></article>
